WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.453
Version everything that changes the result.

2
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:18.520
An eval result is a function of at least five things: the prompt (template and any few-shot examples), the model (an exact id, not an alias), the request settings (effort, max tokens, tools), the dataset (which questions, which reference answers),

3
00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:21.893
and the graders (including the judge model and its prompt).

4
00:00:22.173 --> 00:00:24.264
Change any one and the score can move.

5
00:00:24.544 --> 00:00:27.445
Change two at once and you can’t say which one moved it.

6
00:00:27.725 --> 00:00:34.595
Practical habits: Pin model ids in config, and record the id the API reports in each response.

7
00:00:34.875 --> 00:00:42.512
Anthropic’s models overview lists the current ids; aliases are convenient for prototypes and wrong for a baseline.

8
00:00:42.792 --> 00:00:46.461
Keep prompts in files, in the repository, reviewed like code.

9
00:00:46.741 --> 00:00:48.853
Give each a version or a content hash.

10
00:00:49.133 --> 00:00:50.477
Version the dataset.

11
00:00:50.757 --> 00:00:55.579
Store it as JSONL in the repository (or with a content hash if it’s large).

12
00:00:55.859 --> 00:01:01.939
Never edit a case in place without bumping the version: a “fixed” reference answer is a different eval.

13
00:01:02.219 --> 00:01:11.904
Write a manifest with every run: git commit, prompt hashes, model ids, settings, dataset hash, grader versions, number of trials, cache hit rate, and date.

14
00:01:12.184 --> 00:01:15.107
Results without a manifest can’t be compared later.

15
00:01:15.387 --> 00:01:17.243
Change one thing per comparison.

16
00:01:17.523 --> 00:01:26.013
If you migrate a model and must re-tune the prompt, measure the new model with the old prompt first, then the new prompt, so you know what each change bought.

17
00:01:26.293 --> 00:01:28.320
This is also what makes the gate fair.

18
00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:40.077
The baseline must be the same suite, same dataset version, and same graders as the candidate, rerun under the same conditions, or the paired comparison is pairing different experiments.
